// The network interface class protocol (spec/ARCHITECTURE.md section 7):
// what every network driver provides to the network stack.

/// A network interface: Ethernet frames out and in.
///
/// As with `silica.block.Device`, the calls are the control path. Frames do
/// not travel as calls: they go through a pair of rings in a frame the
/// client shares (`connect`), and lie in frames the client registers
/// (`add_memory`) and the device reads and writes in place. To send, the
/// client puts a frame in its memory and a transmit request in the ring; to
/// receive, it offers buffers ahead of time with receive requests, and each
/// completes when a frame has arrived in it, with the frame's length. The
/// layout of the rings and the records is written out in the `silica_net`
/// crate's documentation, which also has a client.
///
/// A frame is an Ethernet frame from its destination address to the end of
/// its payload, without the frame check sequence: the device adds that and
/// strips it.
///
/// One client is connected at a time. Everything it shares stays its own:
/// it takes the rings and the memory back by revoking them (or by ending).
/// A provider that ends is seen as `peer_gone` on the next call; receive
/// buffers it had not filled are offered again to the provider that
/// replaces it, and transmit requests it had not completed may be sent
/// again (a frame may then go out twice, which networks allow).
protocol silica.net.Device version 1 {
    /// Why a call was refused.
    enum Error : u32 {
        /// Another client is connected, or this one already is.
        busy = 1,
        /// `connect` has not been called.
        not_connected = 2,
        /// The capability is not a frame of a usable size, or the number
        /// is already in use (`add_memory`) or names nothing (`remove_memory`).
        bad_memory = 3,
        /// The provider has no room for more: memory or table space.
        no_room = 4,
        /// Requests that use the memory are still in progress.
        in_use = 5,
    }

    /// What an interface is.
    struct Info {
        /// Its Ethernet address, in the low six bytes, first byte lowest.
        address: u64,
        /// The largest payload a frame carries (1500 on most links); a
        /// frame is at most 14 bytes longer.
        mtu: u32,
        /// Whether the link is up now (see the ring's link records).
        link_up: bool,
    }

    /// Describes the interface. May be called at any time.
    info @1 () -> (info: Info);

    /// Sets up the data path, as `silica.block.Device`'s `connect`.
    connect @2 (rings: cap<Frame>, completions: cap<Notification>) -> (requests: cap<Notification>) error Error;

    /// Registers a frame of the client's as memory requests may use, under
    /// the number `id` (below 64). The device is given it for reading, and
    /// with `writable` for writing too (needed for receive buffers).
    add_memory @3 (id: u32, frame: cap<Frame>, writable: bool) -> () error Error;

    /// Withdraws a registration. The device can no longer reach the frame
    /// when the call returns.
    remove_memory @4 (id: u32) -> () error Error;
}
